  f101: [
    [
      "Will it hurt?",
      "Your period itself does not hurt, but the cramps that can come with it sometimes do — usually a dull ache in your lower tummy or back. For most girls it is mild and a warm patch, a walk or rest helps. If pain ever stops you doing normal things, tell a parent or doctor.",
    ],
    [
      "How much blood is there, really?",
      "It looks like a lot, but a whole period is only about 3 to 5 tablespoons spread over several days. The colour can be bright red, dark brown or in-between — all completely normal.",
    ],
    [
      "Can people tell I am on my period?",
      "No. A slim pad stays hidden under any clothes, and nobody can see or smell it. Change every 4 to 6 hours and you stay fresh and confident all day.",
    ],
    [
      "What if I leak or stain my clothes?",
      "It happens to everyone at some point — it is not embarrassing. Tie a jumper around your waist, change into your spare underwear, and rinse the stain in cold water. Keeping a spare pad and undies in your bag means you are always ready.",
    ],
    [
      "How long until it becomes regular?",
      "For the first one to two years, periods are often irregular — early, late, or skipping a month. That is your body settling into its rhythm. Tracking helps you spot your own pattern.",
    ],
    [
      "Can I still do PE, swim and sleepovers?",
      "Yes to all. You can play sport (a pad with wings stays put), swim (using a tampon or cup), and sleep over (an XL+ pad lasts the night). Your period does not have to pause your life.",
    ],
    [
      "Who can I talk to about it?",
      "A parent, an older sister, a school nurse or a trusted teacher — periods are normal and they have all been there. There is no silly question, and you never have to figure it out alone.",
    ],
  ],
